# 🏆 Day 1: Setup & Git Fundamentals

Congratulations on completing your Day 1 training! You've successfully set up your environment from scratch and learned how to version control your code. 

Here is a quick reference guide to everything we accomplished today so you can review it whenever you need!

---

## 🛠️ 1. Environment Setup
We prepared your Windows machine for Ruby development and version control:
- **Ruby & DevKit:** Installed natively using the official RubyInstaller. This gave you access to the `ruby` command to run scripts.
- **Git:** Installed the Git version control system to track changes in your code.
- **GitHub CLI (`gh`):** Installed the official GitHub command-line tool, which allowed us to securely authenticate your account and create repositories directly from the terminal without messing with SSH keys or manual website clicks.

---

## 💻 2. Your First Ruby Script
We created a simple Ruby script (`hello.rb`) to verify the installation:

```ruby
puts "Hello from Ruby!"
puts "Current time is: #{Time.now}"
puts "This is a test"
```
**How to run it:**
```bash
ruby hello.rb
```

---

## 🐙 3. The Core Git Workflow
This is the most important part of your training today. Every time you make changes to your code, you will use this exact 4-step workflow to save it to your GitHub account.

> [!TIP]
> **Always make sure you are in your project folder (e.g., `cd C:\ruby`) before running these!**

### Step 1: Check your status
See what files you have changed, added, or deleted.
```bash
git status
```
*(Red files are changed but not ready to save. Green files are ready to save).*

### Step 2: Stage your changes
Tell Git which files you want to include in your next save. 
```bash
# Add a specific file
git add hello.rb

# Add ALL changed files in the folder (the period means "everything")
git add . 
```

### Step 3: Commit your changes
Take a "snapshot" of your staged files and attach a descriptive message so you know what you changed.
```bash
git commit -m "Your descriptive message here"
```

### Step 4: Push to GitHub
Upload your saved snapshot to your online GitHub repository.
```bash
git push
```

